  [38;5;153msrc/[39m stays untouched.

  Last thing I need before I can write the design.

  [1mThe fork:[0m the hostname table needs a rule for hostnames it doesn't recognize, and that rule is a safety decision, not
  a detail. Your app is a login form, so "which API did this password go to" has real consequences.

  - [1mUnknown host → production[0m — the common default. Buys: deploy to a new domain, preview URL, or CDN alias and it just
    works. Costs: a misconfigured dev box, a typo'd hostname, or a teammate's local alias silently sends real
    credentials at production. The failure is invisible — everything appears to work.
  - [1mUnknown host → throw at load[0m — fail loud. Buys: an unrecognized environment is impossible to miss; you can never
    accidentally point somewhere you didn't intend. Costs: every new domain requires a code change before the page works
    at all, including preview deploys.
  - [1mUnknown host → dev[0m — inverts the risk. A forgotten production hostname means prod users hit your dev API, which is
    worse than the reverse.

  [1mMy recommendation: unknown → throw[0m, with [38;5;153mlocalhost[39m and [38;5;153m127.0.0.1[39m both mapped to dev. This repo has no deploy pipeline
  and no preview URLs, so the "new domain just works" benefit is hypothetical while the credential-misrouting risk is
  not. If preview deploys show up later, switching the fallback is a one-line change — the direction that's expensive to
  undo is the silent one, because you don't find out you needed the other behavior.

  Now the factual part:
